@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia — examination: coupling an intended state to a strong affect
#
# Opened for prima-materia#141. The claim is the weak half of a section whose
# other half asserts an effect outside the person; this one asserts only that
# the coupling changes the one who does it, and the separation is what makes it
# examinable at all.
#
# The protocol below finds the claim borne out and then finds that being borne
# out costs it most of its content. That combination has no state of its own in
# the vocabulary, and the notes carry it rather than the state.
#

pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier a pm:Positing ;
    rdfs:label "Coupling an intended state to a strong affect shifts the one who couples"@en, "Die Kopplung eines Zielzustands an einen starken Affekt verschiebt den Koppelnden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that binding an intended state to a strong affect shifts what the person attends to, how ready they are to act, how they carry themselves, and where their threshold for risk lies."@en ;
    skos:note "Deliberately the weak reading. The neighbouring claim of the same section holds that the coupled state acts outside its carrier; that one is not examined here and nothing below bears on it. Separating them is the point: the weak reading is testable and the strong one is not, and running them together would let evidence for the first be read as evidence for the second."@en ;
    pm:positedIn "prima-materia#141" .

pmc:AffectCouplingProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the shift occurs, and whether the claim says anything about it"@en, "Ob die Verschiebung eintritt, und ob die Behauptung etwas über sie sagt"@de ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether a strong affect shifts attention, readiness and the threshold for risk, and of what remains of the claim once it does."@en ;
    skos:note "That the shift occurs is not in doubt and the claim is borne out in that narrow sense. Strong affect measurably moves risk judgement and the choices that follow from it, in induced and in naturally occurring form alike."@en ;
    skos:note "What the evidence takes away in the same motion. The direction of the shift is set by the particular affect and not by its strength. Fear and anger are both strong and both unpleasant, and they move the threshold for risk in opposite directions: the fearful judge risk pessimistically and choose cautiously, the angry judge it optimistically and choose boldly, with the angry closer to the cheerful than to the fearful. A claim that names only the strength of the affect therefore cannot say which way anything moves, and is compatible with every outcome."@en ;
    skos:note "On the state. This protocol is the case that made pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate necessary, and it is deliberately not recorded as cases without deviation. There the claim says something and the cases bear it out; here they bear it out because the claim does not say enough to be borne out against anything. Filing it as an ordinary confirmation would have added a success to a record that had in fact found the claim empty."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence for anyone acting on the claim, which is the reason to enter this node at all. Whoever couples an intended state to a strong affect in order to shift his own threshold does not know from the claim whether he has raised or lowered it. Confirmation at this level of generality is not usable guidance, and the node says so rather than recording a success."@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried and not favourable to either side. A registered replication of the key studies in a large online sample supports the framework only in part: the association between the emboldening affect and both risk-seeking and optimistic estimates holds, while the association between the cautioning affect and risk-seeking does not reliably appear. The opposition of directions survives; its symmetry does not."@en ;
    skos:note "A convergence worth following separately. Several transmissions warn against exactly this coupling on the ground that it deceives the practitioner about his own position, and traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl already carries pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle in that shape. If the direction of the shift is unknowable in advance, the warning and the finding say something close to the same thing from opposite ends, and that is a candidate for a convergence node rather than a remark here."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A threshold for risk that has been raised, and one that has been lowered. The claim covers both alike, so acting on it cannot tell the practitioner which of the two he has brought about."@en ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Strong affect, induced or occurring, leaves risk judgement and the choices following from it unmoved against a neutral condition."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The protocol reads the finding off experimental work on specific emotions and their appraisal tendencies, and off a registered replication of it" ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Fear, anger, and risk, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology 81 (2001), no. 1, pp. 146-159, doi:10.1037/0022-3514.81.1.146" , "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Beyond valence: toward a model of emotion-specific influences on judgement and choice, Cognition and Emotion 14 (2000), no. 4, pp. 473-493" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date .
